<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meshchera</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Meshchera were a Finnic people of the forests along the middle Oka river in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, mentioned in medieval Russian sources. Their lands came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and they were gradually absorbed by Russian settlers and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> of the Qasim Khanate, centred on the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasimov">Gorodets Meshchersky</a>. The marshy Meshchera Lowlands east of Moscow still bear their name.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meshchera.jpg" alt="Meshchera" width="320" height="329" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Urutseg; derivative work by Karland90 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Finno-ugrian-map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Meshchera lived between the Oka and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klyazma">Klyazma</a> rivers. The first Russian text to name them is the Tolkovaya Paleya of the 13th century, and later chronicles before the 16th century mention them too, whereas the related <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merya">Merya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muroma">Muroma</a> seem to have been absorbed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavs</a> by the 10th and 11th centuries. Archaeologists identify the Oka-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> culture with them. Burials found at Zhabki in 1870 held women's bronze ornaments of the 5th to 8th centuries, and twelve such sites run from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moskva-river">Moskva River</a> along the Oka to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kasimov">Kasimov</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Slavic tribes reached Meshchera lands in the 10th to 12th centuries. In the Oka valley the Meshchera culture seems to have vanished by the 11th century, without sign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> but with some people pushed away, while in the marshy north, which held less interest for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, they stayed and became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>. In his will of 1358 Ivan II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> mentions the village of Meshcherka, bought from the native chieftain Alexander Ukovich.</p><p>The nobility was converted and assimilated by the 13th century, but hunters and fishermen may have kept their language and beliefs longer: in the 16th century the St Nicholas monastery at Radovitsy was founded to convert the remaining pagans. Documents on Ivan the Terrible's Kazan campaign mention a Meshchera state, Temnikov Meshchera, absorbed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mordvins">Mordvins</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Meshchera were mainly fishermen, beekeepers, hunters and bronze workers who farmed only a little, and they held animistic beliefs before their assimilation. Women's graves contain rings, jingling pendants, buckles, torcs and round breast plates with distinctive ornament. Their language is unattested: Prince A. M. Kurbsky wrote that a Mordvin language was spoken there, and Pauli Rahkonen has suggested from place names that it was Permic, a view Vladimir Napolskikh has criticised. Ethnographers treat the modern Meshchera as a local group of Russians in the forests where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> and Vladimir Oblasts meet, and the Mishar Tatars may have inherited the name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meshchera people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its khans, descendants of Genghis Khan, ruled a Tatar nobility and local <b>Meshchera</b> and Russian subjects, kept their Muslim faith and served in Moscow's armies.</blockquote><a href="/state/qasim-khanate">Qasim Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
